# live-preview-cost — looprs-00u.14
# measured 2026-10-09T07:26:49Z, Darwin 25.6.0 arm64 arm, macOS, nightly rustc, dev + release profiles
#
# The ticket asked for **µs and bytes per frame against the length of the live
# block**, before and after. This file is that measurement, and every number in
# the summary below was read out of the four runs in this file rather than typed.
#
# Harness: src/measure.rs ::
#   the_live_tail_preview_measured_per_frame_against_the_block_it_renders
#
# 527 real answer/thinking entries (1,443 KiB) from 12 real `pi` passes of
# this repo's own beads loop, streamed 128 B per simulated frame into the real
# `SessionView`, in the frame's real order — `flush(width)`, then
# `preview(width)` — with the counting global allocator open across the run.
# 11,821 frames per scenario; the buckets are the live-tail length the flusher
# was standing on (`SessionView::preview_len`), read between the flush and the
# preview.
#
# Scenarios:
#   preview: new bytes arrived  the frame after the entry grew. Unavoidable work.
#   preview: no new bytes       the same call again with nothing changed — the
#                             frame the 16 ms redraw clock asks for between two
#                             deltas, which is most of a streaming run.
#   preview: width changed      same bytes, different window. Must NEVER be free.
#   clone of rendered rows      the floor: handing already-rendered rows over.
#   flush: ...                  the other half of the same frame, for context.
#
# The BEFORE half ran from a pristine copy of HEAD (6ea082a) carrying only a
# read-only `preview_len` accessor on the flusher, so the two halves differ by
# the shipped change and nothing else.
#
# Knob: LOOPRS_MEASURE_CHUNK (bytes per simulated frame, default 128).
#
# Run:
#   LOOPRS_MEASURE_CORPUS=~/.pi/agent/sessions/--Users-danielstahl-Documents-code-ml-personal-harness-pi-beads-looprs-- \
#     cargo test --bin looprs -- --ignored --nocapture --test-threads=1 the_live_tail

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHAT THE NUMBERS SAY
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# 1. THE PREMISE IS HALF WRONG — THE LIVE SLICE IS NOT THE ANSWER.
#    `Cursor::block` restarts at every blank line, so `preview` re-parses the
#    open *paragraph*: p50 119 B, p95 643 B, p99 1,120 B, max 3,039 B — while
#    the entries those paragraphs came from reach 56,362 B. Nothing grows with
#    the answer; the cost grows with a paragraph and then resets. The ticket's
#    "grows with it at exactly the moment the frame budget is tightest" does
#    not happen on this corpus.
#
# 2. ON TIME ALONE THE TICKET'S OWN BAR IS NOT CROSSED.
#    The worst live block measured (2–4 KiB) costs 313 µs mean, 369 µs p95 in
#    the dev profile, 37.5 µs in release. The ticket said: under 1 ms at the
#    worst live block, close with the numbers and no code change. Time alone
#    says exactly that — this was never a frame-budget emergency.
#
# 3. ON BYTES IT IS NOT NEGLIGIBLE, AND BYTES WERE THE OTHER HALF OF THE ASK.
#    One draw of a 2–4 KiB paragraph allocates 91,496 B; pooled over the whole
#    corpus, 18,840 B per preview call. At the 16 ms redraw clock that is
#    ~1.1 MB/s of allocator churn for a single streaming view — ~85 MB over
#    the ~75 s a long answer takes to arrive — and in most of those frames for
#    pixels identical to the last frame's.
#
# 4. THE FRAMES THAT CHANGED NOTHING ARE THE MAJORITY OF A STREAMING RUN.
#    The row animation marks the frame dirty on every tick while the session is
#    busy; deltas arrive in bursts. The harness's deliberately pessimistic 1:1
#    model — one delta per frame, plus one wasted second call — still lands at
#    48.9% of preview calls changing nothing (11,821 of 24,169). A real
#    60 fps stream draws more than one frame per arriving chunk whenever the
#    chunks are further apart than 16 ms, so the real share is higher, not
#    lower.
#
# 5. THE CACHE HITS THE FLOOR IT SET, EXACTLY.
#    After the change a no-new-bytes preview costs 3.9 µs / 1,626 B against the
#    26.5 µs / 18,840 B it cost before — **6.8x less time, 11.6x fewer bytes** — and
#    that is identical to `clone of rendered rows (floor)` in every bucket,
#    which is what a hit is: rows that were already rendered, handed over.
#    Dev profile, by live-tail bucket:
#
#      live tail      before              after              time     bytes
#      <256 B           12.9 µs /  14.4 KB      1.9 µs /   0.7 KB   6.8x   21.2x
#      256 B–1 KiB      56.6 µs /  27.5 KB      8.4 µs /   3.6 KB   6.7x    7.6x
#      1–2 KiB         166.6 µs /  53.6 KB     24.2 µs /  10.9 KB   6.9x    4.9x
#      2–4 KiB         312.6 µs /  89.4 KB     45.0 µs /  20.5 KB   6.9x    4.4x
#
#    Release profile, pooled: 2.9 µs → 0.6 µs, same bytes curve.
#
# 6. THE PRICE IS ON THE FRAMES THAT CARRY NEWS, AND IT IS ONE CLONE.
#    A miss now costs what it cost plus the clone handed back and the `src` copy
#    kept for the byte comparison: pooled dev 32.9 µs / 20,527 B against 27.3 µs /
#    18,855 B — **+5.6 µs (+21%) and +1,672 B** on a news-carrying frame,
#    against **−22.6 µs and −17,214 B** on an unchanged one. Pairing one of each — the
#    harness's 1:1 mix — a (grew, unchanged) pair costs 36.8 µs after and
#    53.8 µs before: **32% off the streaming frame bill**. Break-even is
#    one clone per ~4 unchanged frames; anything worse than a delta
#    arriving every third frame is a win, and a stream that produced new bytes
#    on every single frame would be a loss of one clone per frame.
#
# 7. A WIDTH CHANGE STILL RENDERS: 15.8 µs → 19.2 µs, a full parse at
#    the new width in both directions. That is the invariant ADR-0002 Q5 needs
#    — a hit across a width would make the rows that *sized* the live pane
#    describe a different window than the rows that *fill* it. Seven tests in
#    `src/components/line_render.rs::tests` pin that, and pin staleness against
#    a changed tail, a changed entry style, a finished entry, the buffer cap
#    eating the head of the live entry, a reseat, and the raw/fence branch
#    change — each asserting the hit/miss counter rather than a stopwatch.
#
# 8. THE `flush` COLUMN IS CONTEXT, AND ITS OUTLIERS ARE NOT THIS TICKET'S.
#    The slowest flush frames (worst 78 ms dev, 5 ms release) all land on
#    the frame where a **fenced code block first opens**: syntect compiling a
#    syntax context on first highlight, which is `looprs-00u.16`'s finding,
#    caught here as a by-product. The evidence is the harness's own warm-up:
#    the first run warmed only `rust` and frame f304 cost 296 ms; warming the
#    other languages the corpus fences in (`bash`/`sh`/`python`/`json`/`text`)
#    moved that same frame to 78 ms. The live-tail path never highlights — a
#    live fence hands over the raw line, ADR-0001 rule 1 — so no preview number
#    in this file contains a syntax compile.
#
# KEPT, given point 2. The measurement did not find the quadratic the ticket
# expected; it found a flat cost being paid twice — once for the frame that had
# news, and again for the frame that did not. The change removes
# 95–77% of the per-frame bytes on frames that need no new pixels, and
# 6.8x of the dev-profile preview time, for one clone on the frames that
# really do have news.

